==Catalan==

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|ca|roa-oca|son}}, from {{inh|ca|VL.||sum}}, reduced form of {{inh|ca|la|suus|suum}}, accusative of {{m|la|suus}}, from {{inh|ca|itc-pro|*sowos}}. Compare {{cog|oc,fr|son}}.

In unstressed position in Vulgar Latin {{m|la||suum, suam}} etc. were monosyllabic and regularly became {{m|ca||son, sa}} etc. in Catalan. When stressed they were disyllabic and became {{m|ca|seu}}, {{m|roa-oca|sua}} > {{m|ca|seua}} etc.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|ca|/sun/|a=Balearic,Central}} {{q|always unstressed}}
* {{IPA|ca|/son/|a=Valencian}} {{q|always unstressed}}

====Determiner====
{{head|ca|determiner|cat2=possessive determiners|g=m|feminine|sa|masculine plural|sos|feminine plural|ses}}

# [[his]], [[her]], [[its]]
# [[their]]
# [[your]] (alluding to {{m|ca|vostè}} or {{m|ca|vostès}})

=====Usage notes=====
* {{ca-poss-determiner-note}}

The standard masculine plural form is ''sos'', but {{m|ca|sons}} can be found in some dialects.

In Algherese, ''son'' and its forms mainly give reference to ''vostè''.

